From fa66c0f8a418252119d9ee4673c4000b340f2999 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:52:13 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [meta] warn on calls to functions without specification in
 reading/writing contexts

A call to an undefined function is checked through the callee's assigns
(writing) or \from (reading) clauses. A behavior without assigns gives the
warning "Cannot analyze footprint of function f: no assigns clause", but a
function declared without any specification has no behavior at all, so the
call was skipped silently. This is the case of every libc function when the
code is parsed with the system headers (-no-frama-c-stdlib, the default of
e-acsl-gcc): memcpy(&protected, ...) gets no instance and no message.

Emit the same warning in that case, and give both a warning category,
no-footprint, so that it can be silenced or made an error.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
